# Fake News Detection Using Machine Learning

An end-to-end Machine Learning project designed to classify news articles as either reliable or fake using Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques and supervised classification models.

## 📌 Project Overview
The proliferation of misinformation online highlights the critical need for automated verification systems. This project applies textual preprocessing and feature extraction methods to analyze structural patterns in news text, training a predictive model to identify deceptive content with high accuracy.

## 🚀 Key Features & Workflow
- **Data Preprocessing**: Implemented tokenization, stop-word removal, lemmatization, and lowercasing to clean raw text inputs.
- **Feature Engineering**: Utilized TF-IDF (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency) vectorization to convert textual data into meaningful numerical matrices.
- **Model Training**: Evaluated multiple classifiers including Logistic Regression and Random Forest to benchmark performance.
- **Evaluation**: Assessed the model using standard metrics including Accuracy, Confusion Matrix, Precision, and Recall.

## 📊 Performance & Results
- **Best Performing Model**: Passive Aggressive Classifier / Random Forest
- **Training Accuracy**: ~93-95%
- **Key Visualizations**: Evaluation outputs, model loss curves, and feature importances are automatically logged and saved within the `/results` folder.

## 🛠️ Tech Stack & Libraries
- **Language**: Python
- **Libraries**: Pandas, NumPy, Scikit-Learn, NLTK, Matplotlib, Seaborn

## 📂 Project Structure
```text
├── data/                  # Raw dataset files (ignored in version control)
├── results/               # Saved plots, confusion matrices, and performance metrics
├── Fake_News_Detection.ipynb   # Main Jupyter notebook containing EDA and training
├── requirements.txt       # File listing dependencies for simple replication
└── README                 # Project documentation
```

## 🎯 Sample Interview Questions Answered by This Project
- **"How did you handle textual features?"**: Transformed raw text data into numerical features using TF-IDF Vectorization to reflect word importance while minimizing common filler words.
- **"Why split the dataset?"**: Used an 80/20 train-test split to ensure rigorous evaluation against unseen data, preventing overfitting.
